WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.417
Client Meetings.

2
00:00:01.697 --> 00:00:05.878
Day 71 of the Soft Skill Playbook course, in the Workplace phase.

3
00:00:07.628 --> 00:00:08.652
The key idea.

4
00:00:08.932 --> 00:00:11.727
The client is buying confidence as much as software.

5
00:00:12.007 --> 00:00:18.513
Prepare well, listen more than you talk, never promise what you can't deliver, and always leave with clear next steps.

6
00:00:19.863 --> 00:00:20.717
Part 1.

7
00:00:20.997 --> 00:00:22.703
The client meeting lifecycle.

8
00:00:23.953 --> 00:00:24.871
Step 1:

9
00:00:25.151 --> 00:00:28.991
Prepare: goal, agenda, attendees, their context, your numbers.

10
00:00:29.271 --> 00:00:30.188
Step 2:

11
00:00:30.468 --> 00:00:33.156
Open: introductions, agenda, time check.

12
00:00:33.436 --> 00:00:34.353
Step 3:

13
00:00:34.633 --> 00:00:38.964
Discover or Present: ask questions, then show progress or solution.

14
00:00:39.244 --> 00:00:40.204
Step 4:

15
00:00:40.484 --> 00:00:44.132
Handle concerns: listen, acknowledge, answer, confirm.

16
00:00:44.412 --> 00:00:45.372
Step 5:

17
00:00:45.652 --> 00:00:48.020
Close: recap decisions, owners, dates.

18
00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:49.239
Step 6:

19
00:00:49.519 --> 00:00:52.100
Follow up: minutes within 24 hours.

20
00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:54.325
Part 2.

21
00:00:54.605 --> 00:00:56.354
Before the meeting: checklist.

22
00:00:57.604 --> 00:01:00.399
Goal: what must be decided or agreed by the end?

23
00:01:00.679 --> 00:01:02.855
Agenda sent at least a day in advance.

24
00:01:03.135 --> 00:01:05.780
Who's attending, and their roles (LinkedIn check).

25
00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:12.524
Internal alignment: agree with your manager or account manager on who says what, and what we will not commit to.

26
00:01:12.804 --> 00:01:17.369
Demo tested on the same network and device you'll use, plus a backup recording.

27
00:01:17.649 --> 00:01:21.361
Numbers ready: progress %, timelines, open issues.

28
00:01:22.711 --> 00:01:26.807
Likely questions and your answers (especially about delays and cost).

29
00:01:28.157 --> 00:01:29.053
Part 3.

30
00:01:29.333 --> 00:01:30.464
Opening the meeting.

31
00:01:31.714 --> 00:01:32.653
You could say:

32
00:01:32.933 --> 00:01:35.471
Good morning, everyone, and thanks for your time.

33
00:01:35.751 --> 00:01:38.077
I'm Ashish, the tech lead for the mobile app.

34
00:01:38.357 --> 00:01:46.421
Today we'd like to cover three things: sprint progress, a demo of the new checkout, and two decisions we need from you on payment options.

35
00:01:46.701 --> 00:01:48.215
We have 45 minutes.

36
00:01:48.495 --> 00:01:50.159
Does that agenda work for you?

37
00:01:51.509 --> 00:01:52.427
Part 4.

38
00:01:52.707 --> 00:01:54.925
Discovery: understand what they need.

39
00:01:56.175 --> 00:01:57.541
Here is a quick table:

40
00:01:57.821 --> 00:01:59.143
Question, and Why.

41
00:01:59.423 --> 00:02:01.855
What does success look like for you in 3 months?:

42
00:02:02.135 --> 00:02:03.458
Learn their real goal.

43
00:02:03.738 --> 00:02:06.127
What's the biggest pain point for your users today?:

44
00:02:06.407 --> 00:02:07.687
Find priorities.

45
00:02:07.967 --> 00:02:09.589
Who else needs to approve this?:

46
00:02:09.869 --> 00:02:11.298
Know the decision-makers.

47
00:02:11.578 --> 00:02:13.903
What happens if this launches a month later?:

48
00:02:14.183 --> 00:02:16.125
Find out how firm the deadline is.

49
00:02:16.405 --> 00:02:20.629
Are there any constraints we should know about: budget, compliance, systems?:

50
00:02:20.909 --> 00:02:22.210
Avoid surprises.

51
00:02:22.490 --> 00:02:23.983
How do you measure that today?:

52
00:02:24.263 --> 00:02:25.287
Get a baseline.

53
00:02:26.637 --> 00:02:27.277
Tip:

54
00:02:27.557 --> 00:02:30.160
Talk 30%, listen 70%.

55
00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:31.720
Take notes visibly.

56
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:33.451
Repeat back key points:

57
00:02:33.731 --> 00:02:36.803
So the must-have for launch is UPI and COD.

58
00:02:37.083 --> 00:02:38.427
Wallets can come later.

59
00:02:38.707 --> 00:02:39.517
Correct?

60
00:02:40.867 --> 00:02:41.827
Part 5.

61
00:02:42.107 --> 00:02:43.515
Scripts for tough moments.

62
00:02:44.765 --> 00:02:45.960
Reporting a delay.

63
00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:47.179
You could say:

64
00:02:47.459 --> 00:02:49.208
I want to be transparent with you.

65
00:02:49.488 --> 00:02:55.184
The payment integration will be 1 week later than planned: the 22nd instead of the 15th.

66
00:02:55.464 --> 00:02:57.683
The bank changed their API last week.

67
00:02:57.963 --> 00:02:59.541
Everything else is on track.

68
00:02:59.821 --> 00:03:05.048
To reduce the impact, we can release the rest of the app on the 15th with COD only.

69
00:03:05.328 --> 00:03:06.523
Would that work for you?

70
00:03:07.873 --> 00:03:12.374
Tell them early, explain the cause in one line, give a new date, and offer an option.

71
00:03:13.724 --> 00:03:15.708
Client asks for something out of scope.

72
00:03:15.988 --> 00:03:16.927
You could say:

73
00:03:17.207 --> 00:03:20.620
That's a great idea, and I can see how it helps your sales team.

74
00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:25.337
It's outside the current scope, so I don't want to promise it without checking the impact.

75
00:03:25.617 --> 00:03:33.617
I'll come back by Thursday with an estimate and options: either adding it as a change request, or swapping it for something lower-priority.

76
00:03:34.967 --> 00:03:36.269
You don't know the answer.

77
00:03:36.549 --> 00:03:37.487
You could say:

78
00:03:37.767 --> 00:03:38.770
Good question.

79
00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:45.770
I don't want to give you a wrong answer, so let me confirm with our security team and get back to you by tomorrow evening.

80
00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:49.061
Client is unhappy or angry.

81
00:03:49.341 --> 00:03:50.280
You could say:

82
00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:55.083
I understand, and I'm sorry this affected your customers during the weekend sale.

83
00:03:55.363 --> 00:03:56.813
You're right to be concerned.

84
00:03:57.093 --> 00:04:01.808
Here's what happened, what we've already fixed, and what we're changing so it doesn't happen again.

85
00:04:02.088 --> 00:04:05.565
I'll personally send you a daily update until it's fully closed.

86
00:04:06.915 --> 00:04:10.393
Don't argue, don't blame the client's team, don't get defensive.

87
00:04:10.673 --> 00:04:12.955
Acknowledge, take ownership, give a plan.

88
00:04:14.305 --> 00:04:16.673
Client pushes for an unrealistic deadline.

89
00:04:16.953 --> 00:04:17.892
You could say:

90
00:04:18.172 --> 00:04:20.433
We want to hit that date as much as you do.

91
00:04:20.713 --> 00:04:24.575
With the current scope, the earliest high-quality release is the 30th.

92
00:04:24.855 --> 00:04:31.084
To launch on the 15th, we could go live with the core ordering flow and add loyalty points two weeks later.

93
00:04:31.364 --> 00:04:35.119
Which matters more for your launch: the date or the full feature set?

94
00:04:36.469 --> 00:04:38.474
Saying no to a technical demand.

95
00:04:38.754 --> 00:04:39.693
You could say:

96
00:04:39.973 --> 00:04:43.023
I understand why storing card details looks simpler.

97
00:04:43.303 --> 00:04:44.839
We'd recommend against it.

98
00:04:45.119 --> 00:04:50.303
It would bring your app under full PCI-DSS compliance, which adds months and cost.

99
00:04:50.583 --> 00:04:56.194
Tokenisation via the payment gateway gives you the same user experience with none of that risk.

100
00:04:57.544 --> 00:04:58.483
Part 6.

101
00:04:58.763 --> 00:04:59.915
Closing the meeting.

102
00:05:01.165 --> 00:05:02.103
You could say:

103
00:05:02.383 --> 00:05:03.685
Let me quickly recap.

104
00:05:03.965 --> 00:05:08.231
Decisions: we launch with UPI and COD; wallets in phase 2.

105
00:05:08.511 --> 00:05:15.551
Actions: we share the updated timeline by Wednesday; your team sends the bank sandbox credentials by Friday.

106
00:05:15.831 --> 00:05:16.834
Next meeting:

107
00:05:17.114 --> 00:05:18.351
Monday at 11.

108
00:05:18.631 --> 00:05:19.869
Did I miss anything?

109
00:05:21.219 --> 00:05:22.157
Part 7.

110
00:05:22.437 --> 00:05:25.019
Follow-up email (within 24 hours).

111
00:05:26.269 --> 00:05:28.146
Here is a template you can copy.

112
00:05:28.426 --> 00:05:29.301
Subject:

113
00:05:29.581 --> 00:05:32.461
Minutes, Sprint 6 review Decisions and next steps.

114
00:05:32.741 --> 00:05:33.743
Hi Rohan.

115
00:05:34.023 --> 00:05:35.538
Thanks for your time today.

116
00:05:35.818 --> 00:05:36.863
Summary below:

117
00:05:37.143 --> 00:05:38.061
Decisions.

118
00:05:38.341 --> 00:05:42.159
Launch with UPI + COD; wallets moved to phase 2.

119
00:05:42.439 --> 00:05:43.506
Action items.

120
00:05:43.786 --> 00:05:46.538
Us Updated timeline, Ashish, Wed 6 Nov.

121
00:05:46.818 --> 00:05:50.658
You Bank sandbox credentials, Rohan's team, Fri 8 Nov.

122
00:05:50.938 --> 00:05:53.007
The rest of the template is on the page.

123
00:05:55.857 --> 00:05:56.732
Part 8.

124
00:05:57.012 --> 00:05:58.143
Client etiquette.

125
00:05:59.393 --> 00:06:00.758
Here is a quick table:

126
00:06:01.038 --> 00:06:02.041
Do, and Don't.

127
00:06:02.321 --> 00:06:04.731
Join 2 to 3 minutes early, camera on:

128
00:06:05.011 --> 00:06:07.571
Argue with your own team in front of the client.

129
00:06:07.851 --> 00:06:11.094
Use the client's terms (orders, not OrderAggregate):

130
00:06:11.374 --> 00:06:14.403
Criticise the client's existing systems or vendors.

131
00:06:14.683 --> 00:06:16.817
Agree internally on who answers what:

132
00:06:17.097 --> 00:06:19.507
Commit to dates or features without checking.

133
00:06:19.787 --> 00:06:21.665
Write down every promise you make:

134
00:06:21.945 --> 00:06:24.547
Discuss internal problems or other clients.

135
00:06:24.827 --> 00:06:27.110
Say we, not they (for your own company):

136
00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:30.590
Use heavy jargon or acronyms without explaining them.

137
00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:32.836
Part 9.

138
00:06:33.116 --> 00:06:34.887
Beginner versus experienced.

139
00:06:36.137 --> 00:06:36.969
Beginner.

140
00:06:37.249 --> 00:06:39.361
You may be there to listen and take notes.

141
00:06:39.641 --> 00:06:41.603
Do that brilliantly; it's valued.

142
00:06:41.883 --> 00:06:45.510
Speak when asked about your area: short, factual, confident.

143
00:06:45.790 --> 00:06:48.030
If unsure whether you can share something, say:

144
00:06:48.310 --> 00:06:50.273
Let me check with Priya and confirm.

145
00:06:51.623 --> 00:06:52.711
Experienced.

146
00:06:52.991 --> 00:06:57.812
Pre-wire the meeting: call the client's key person beforehand about anything sensitive.

147
00:06:58.092 --> 00:07:00.652
Bring options with trade-offs, not just status.

148
00:07:00.932 --> 00:07:06.095
Build the relationship beyond projects: ask about their goals, their pressures, their boss.

149
00:07:06.375 --> 00:07:09.468
Protect your team from scope creep, politely and firmly.

150
00:07:10.818 --> 00:07:11.671
Part 10.

151
00:07:11.951 --> 00:07:13.338
Practice conversation:

152
00:07:13.618 --> 00:07:15.367
Telling a client about a delay.

153
00:07:16.617 --> 00:07:18.644
Here is how the conversation goes.

154
00:07:18.924 --> 00:07:21.100
So, are we still on track for the 15th?

155
00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:27.225
I want to be transparent: the payment integration will be one week late, on the 22nd.

156
00:07:27.505 --> 00:07:29.937
The bank changed their API last week.

157
00:07:30.217 --> 00:07:31.583
That's disappointing.

158
00:07:31.863 --> 00:07:33.996
Our marketing launch is on the 15th.

159
00:07:34.276 --> 00:07:35.641
I understand.

160
00:07:35.921 --> 00:07:37.969
Everything else is on track.

161
00:07:38.249 --> 00:07:44.948
We can go live on the 15th with cash on delivery and UPI, and add cards on the 22nd.

162
00:07:46.298 --> 00:07:47.898
The conversation continues.

163
00:07:48.178 --> 00:07:49.885
Would that work for customers?

164
00:07:50.165 --> 00:07:50.869
Yes.

165
00:07:51.149 --> 00:07:55.095
About 80% of your orders are UPI or cash today.

166
00:07:55.375 --> 00:07:56.890
Okay, let's do that.

167
00:07:57.170 --> 00:07:59.175
Please send me the updated plan.

168
00:07:59.455 --> 00:08:01.823
I'll send it today, by 6 pm.

169
00:08:03.173 --> 00:08:04.709
That's the end of this lesson.

170
00:08:04.989 --> 00:08:09.661
Pick one script from it, say it out loud three times, and use it for real today.

171
00:08:09.941 --> 00:08:16.043
For extra practice, paste the Master Prompt into an AI assistant and role-play the conversation.

